<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dacians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dacians were an ancient people of the Carpathian region and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and neighbouring lands, closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> and the Getae. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista</a> united them in a powerful kingdom in the 1st century BCE, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decebalus">Decebalus</a> they fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> conquered them in 106 CE and created the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dacians.jpg" alt="Dacians" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laci3 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dacio_seg%C3%BAn_modelo_del_Foro_de_Trajano_-_Museo_del_Prado.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A cult found in Thrace, Moesia, Phrygia and the lands of the <b>Dacians</b> and Getae honoured the “Thracian horseman”, also known as Sabazios or the Thracian Heros, by a Thracian name Heros Karabazmos, a god of the underworld usually shown on funeral monuments as a rider slaying a…</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The triumphal column in Trajan's Forum commemorates the emperor's victory in the Dacian Wars, and its spiral bas relief depicts the wars between Romans and <b>Dacians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-12">12 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the 5th century BCE they vanish from the record; their lands later appear as part of the territory of the Getae and <b>Dacians</b>, into whom they were probably absorbed.</blockquote><a href="/state/agathyrsi">Agathyrsi</a></li><li><blockquote>About 82 BCE Burebista united the Getae and <b>Dacians</b> into the Kingdom of Dacia; Roman writers afterwards used Getae and Dacians almost interchangeably.</blockquote><a href="/state/getae">Getae</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome alternately paid and fought them, using them against the <b>Dacians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/iazyges">Iazyges</a></li></ul></section>
